    tolerable.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m lead time, then due. The warning is the notification that has real value, and stopping at the
  deadline avoids building snooze and nag-suppression UI in v1. Overdue nagging is additive later — it reads the same
  fields.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · When should a task's reminder fire? → Lead time, then due (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Next, the edge case that defines a time-driven system which only runs while the tab is open: reminders that came due
  while the app was closed.

  [1mThe fork:[0m what the app does on startup when it finds reminders whose moment has passed. This is a user-facing default,
  cheap to change, but it determines whether the "fired" bookkeeping happens at startup or only on the live timer.

  - [1mFire everything missed[0m — open the app after a week and get eleven notifications at once. Honest, but a flood, and
    the ones for tasks already past due are just noise.
  - [1mOne summary on reopen[0m — collapse them: "3 reminders fired while you were away," linking to the tasks. Marks them all
    fired so they don't repeat. Keeps the signal, drops the flood.
  - [1mSuppress missed, rely on the list[0m — don't notify at all for anything in the past; just let overdue tasks render as
    overdue in the task list. Quietest, but a reminder you were away for vanishes entirely.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m one summary on reopen. It preserves the thing you actually wanted — being told — without punishing
  you for having closed the tab, and overdue styling in the list covers the ongoing state.
